    },{
      "title_s":["EFFECT OF ANTITRANSPIRANTS ON GROWTH, YIELD, ITS COMPONENTS AND WATER PRODUCTIVITY OF SUNFLOWER UNDER WATER STRESS CONDITION"],
      "abstract_s":["A field experiment was carried out at Itay El-Baroud Agricultural Research Station, El-Behera Governorate, Egypt to study the effect of three levels of water treatments, i.e. full irrigation: 100% ETc (crop evapotranspiration), moderate stress: 75% ETc and high stress: 50% ETc and three antitranspirants, i.e. kaolin, MgCO3 and flaxseed oil on growth, yield, its components, some physiological traits and water productivity of two sunflower cultivars, i.e. Sakha 53 and Giza 102 during the two growing seasons of 2017 and 2018. Results indicated that increasing soil moisture stress up to 50% ETc significantly decreased (p˂0.05) sunflower cultivars growth, yield, its components and some physiological traits. However, leaf temperature and proline content significantly increased (p˂0.05) when plants exposed to high stress treatment compared to full or moderate treatments. Sakha 53 cv. exceeded Giza 102 in shoot dry weight, seed and oil yields ha-1, yield components, chlorophyll a, b, a+b and seed protein. Sunflowers plants sprayed with antitranspirants under water stress significantly increased (p˂0.05) growth, yield, its components, some physiological traits and irrigation water productivity (IWP) compared with untreated plants. Results also indicated that lower yield reductions (RYD %) were recorded from spraying Sakha 53 by MgCO3 under moderate stress in the first and second seasons. It could be concluded that kaolin or MgCO3 application can ameliorate negative effects of water stress on sunflower growth, yield, its components, seed quality and increased water productivity."],
      "halId_s":"hal-05161178",
      "uri_s":"https://hal.science/hal-05161178v1",
      "version_i":1,
      "submittedDate_s":"2025-07-14 13:19:19",
      "producedDate_s":"2021-11-09"
    },{
      "title_s":["Weather Parameter’s Impact on Population Fluctuation of Insect Pests in Sunflower"],
      "abstract_s":["The population trend of insect pest of sunflower was studied at North bank plain zone of river Brahmaputra, Biswanath district in the state of Assam during 2021-2022. The simple correlation study showed bright sunshine hour had a positive and significant correlation with the whitefly population relative humidity had a positive and significant correlation with the Aphis gossypii and Amrasca biguttula biguttula Ishida population; A. gossypii had positive and significant relation with maximum temperature and Spilosoma obliqua had negative and significant relation with relative humidity."],
      "halId_s":"hal-05160767",
      "uri_s":"https://hal.science/hal-05160767v1",
      "version_i":1,
      "submittedDate_s":"2025-07-14 09:21:01",
      "producedDate_s":"2023-03-13"
    },{
      "title_s":["Effect of Nitrogen and Spacing on Growth and Yield of Sunflower (Helianthus annuus L.)"],
      "abstract_s":["A field experiment was carried out during kharif season of 2022, at crop research farm of Department of Agronomy at Sam Higginbottom University of Agriculture, Technology and Sciences, Prayagraj in North Eastern plains of Eastern Uttar Pradesh, India with the objective to study the effect of nitrogen and spacing on growth, yield and economics of Sunflower (Helianthus annuus L.)with 10 treatments of which treatments (T1-T9) with different combination of nitrogen along with spacing and T10The soil experimental plot was sandy loam in texture, nearly neutral in soil reaction (pH 7.1) The result revealed that application of 70 kg N/ha + 55 X 20 cm recorded Maximum plant height (148.48 cm), plant dry weight (60.53 g), test weight (41.40 g), capitulum diameter (15.13 cm)."],
